Chrome proof is the follow-on gate after that passes. Plan + research: [[2026-06-06 2123 — Doc-driven Jitter B2 plan with sub-agents (019e9fd7)]].\n- Carrier reputation (~45% reject) — vendor problem, not engineering.\n- **Current engineering front: Telnyx provider seam.** Twilio account suspended (KYC), so the\n  carrier is being swapped. A provider-abstraction layer is built and reconciled to Telnyx\'s\n  real API shapes — Phases A/B (seam + Telnyx backend + browser operator), R (schema\n  reconciliation: AMD `machine`, CallSid-from-webhook, 16-route build fix), C0 (exhaustive\n  tests) — on branch `codex/jitter-provider-abstraction`, `next build` green, 1092 unit tests.\n  Live validation PASSED 2026-06-04; current gate is the B2 trust-boundary/readiness/single-line dial order, then Railway worker proof.\n- **New official-docs-first finish-out plan:** [[2026-06-06 2123 — Doc-driven Jitter B2 plan with sub-agents (019e9fd7)]]. Immediate implication: the current failing operator TeXML auth test may be enforcing callback-style signature rules on the wrong boundary. The order is now trust-boundary fix first, then browser-audio readiness contract, then single-line live dial, then real Railway worker proof.\n\n## Integrations\n- Outbound → [[Sandra]]: emit `sandra.writeback`/terminal disposition (normalized call outcome, disposition, batch item, optional callback/recording metadata) → Sandra `call_activities`.\n\n## Known issues / notes\n- ⚠️ **Naming is inverted — footgun.** Supabase project `jitter-test` (`ypgntjjrrutjsratdwmk`) is the **REAL production** data plane; `jitter` (`sbidltfdktstzboivavv`) is **stale/dead**. Easy to nuke the wrong one — rename or document before anyone touches it.\n- Recording pipeline (PR #141) is **sound** — capture→enqueue→upload→hydrate→playback verified; recordings only created on *answered* calls. `K=3` dialing is correct.\n- **Recording happy path is queued behind Phase 7 batch validation** — once batch_id is issued and auth blockers resolved (Supabase Auth whitelist + Vercel log CLI), run a real predictive batch and verify answered calls produce recordings through to playback. Source: [[2026-05-31 0129 — Jitter dialing and recordings review (f6ae0a63)]]; superseded as immediate front by 2026-06-01 Phase 7 captures.\n- **Official-docs-first boundaries for the active lane:** [[Telnyx]] callback signing and [[Telnyx]] TeXML instruction fetching are not the same trust boundary;\n\n### CURATED: projects/Sandra.md\n---\ntype: project\nstatus: active\ntags: [crm, sandra]\ncreated: 2026-05-30\nupdated: 2026-06-07\naliases: [Sandra CRM]\nrelated: ["[[MOC - v1]]", "[[Jitter]]"]\n---\n# Sandra\n\nCRM for BMH. Next.js 16 + Supabase + Vercel. **Live** (~1,400 prospects).\nRepo: `~/Sites/BMH apps/Sandra`\n\n## v1 happy path\nImport prospects → filter to a target list → run an SMS/email sequence → handle replies.\n\n## Browser V1 exhaustive test pass\n- 2026-06-02 prompt handed to Nietzsche: execute `$HOME/BMH-OS/tmp/sandra-v1-test-plan-MERGED.md` as a real-browser Sandra/RCRM pass.\n- Method: drive a real browser like a human for route/persona/filter/write-path proof; read rendered UI, toasts, real console/network, and screenshots. Do not use Playwright/CLI/curl/scripts to exercise or assert features.\n- Narrow exceptions: data plumbing only for 10 browser users, `SANDRA-BROWSER-V1` / `PROD-CANARY` rows, DB-oracle filter counts, and `resetTenantTables()` teardown; authorization probes run from browser DevTools console against the live logged-in session.\n- Deliverable: `$HOME/BMH-OS/tmp/sandra-v1-test-RESULTS.md` with 1Password refs only, browser-backed matrices, redacted provider ledger, cleanup status, defects, and verdict.\n- 2026-06-02T08:23Z controller-owned Chrome update: >250-row UI-created import/job marker is closed. Admin Owner used Chrome/DevTools to select Add Data, upload `/private/tmp/sandra-browser-v1-import-260.csv`, select DealMachine/Jackson County MO, review `260 of 260` valid rows, start the durable job without SMS/provider send, and verify job `/jobs/bbc09186-0d2d-4250-be8b-356d14d94e28` completed `260/260` with `260` succeeded, `0` failed, `0` skipped. Evidence: `/private/tmp/sandra-v1-browser-evidence/059-*` through `071-*`. Overall Browser V1 verdict remains PARTIAL pending filter/persona/mobile/realtime/reset matrix.\n- 2026-06-02T08:43Z controller-owned Chrome update: No Membership and Cross Org visibility slices are closed. No Membership auth fixture was repaired through approved service-role setup, then dashboard/properties/leads/messages rendered zero/empty states with no imported BMH rows. Cross Org dashboard/properties rendered zero/empty states after the merge RPC fix. A no-org Quick Filters UI defect was found and patched in `src/app/(dashboard)/properties/_components/quick-filters-bar.tsx`; no-org `/properties` now renders empty prospects without `Failed to load Quick Filters`; `npm run typecheck -- --pretty false` passed. Overall Browser V1 remains PARTIAL pending filter matrix, write-path/realtime/mobile/reset slices and merge/deploy of the no-org fix.\n- 2026-06-02T08:53Z controller code/PR update: no-org Quick Filters fix was isolated on fresh branch `codex/sandra-no-org-quickfilters`, verified with `npm run typecheck -- --pretty false`, `npm run verify`, and Husky pre-commit `npm run verify`, then merged as PR #223. Remote checks passed: Playwright golden paths `26808897046`, Vercel preview, and Vercel Preview Comments. Squash merge `295b9a17fb458d502ddf417dc1b116b9ca22d1c6`; remote feature branch deleted. No-org Quick Filters marker is closed in code; Browser V1 remains PARTIAL pending filter matrix, write-path/realtime/mobile/reset, and cleanup proof.\n- 2026-06-02T09:07Z controller watchdog update: Hooke completed non-browser prep for the next Admin Owner `/properties` filter visual/DB-oracle slice. Controller remediated the local server blocker by restarting Sandra on `localhost:3457` in detached screen session `sandra-v1-dev-3457` with the existing test env mapped to the middleware\'s expected public Supabase env names; `/properties` now returns a login redirect instead of 500. Browser V1 remains PARTIAL pending controller-owned Chrome login/navigation for filter matrix evidence `088-*` through `095-*`, then write-path/realtime/mobile/reset slices.\n- 2026-06-02T09:14Z controller-owned Chrome update: Admin Owner `/properties` filter visual/DB-oracle slice completed. Evidence `088-*` through `094-*` plus\n\n### CURATED: mocs/MOC - v1 Verification Steps.md\n---\ntype: moc\nstatus: active\ntags: [v1, sandra, jitter, closer-lab, institute]\ncreated: 2026-06-01\nupdated: 2026-06-03\nrelated: ["[[MOC - v1]]", "[[_Active]]", "[[Sandra]]", "[[Jitter]]", "[[Closer Lab]]", "[[BMH Institute]]"]\n---\n# MOC - v1 Verification Steps\n\nDetailed happy-path **+ negative + edge** checklists — the step-by-step detail behind [[MOC - v1]]\'s boxes. The MOC is the scoreboard (one box per app); this is the browser-by-browser expansion. Tick a box when that step passes (`PASS` / `FAIL` / `BLOCKED` / `UNTESTED`).\n\n**Each app section is grouped by dimension:** 1) golden path, 2) auth/role boundaries, 3) bad input, 4) provider failures, 5) duplicate/replay, 6) concurrency/races, 7) empty/limit states, 8) interruptions, 9) integrity/flow-specific, 10+) cleanup. Steps are grounded in the actual codebase (file/route citations); `(UNKNOWN …)` marks where code didn\'t reveal the answer — those are themselves things to verify.\n\n**Principle:** agents map *where to look*; Browser V1 acceptance proves *what works for a VA* through Chrome/DevTools real-browser clicks/navigation and visible UI outcomes. Browser V1 data creation is also part of the browser proof: create the fixture/content/test records through Chrome wherever the UI supports creation or editing. Playwright automation, SQL, shell, unit/integration tests, GitHub Actions, and direct database/API population are supporting evidence only; they do not satisfy Browser V1 acceptance by themselves.\n\nSource: parallel read-only recon agents (2026-06-01) over each repo at `~/Sites/BMH apps/`; original capture [[2026-06-01 1653 — Four-app v1 browser verification approach]].\n\n> ⚠️ **Recon flagged real v1 risks** (verify before calling done): Jitter\'s **Sandra writeback is stubbed/skipped** (observed in audit, not POSTed). **Institute** never generates a certificate PDF (`pdf_path` stays `\'pending\'`) and cert PDF remains deferred post-v1. Resolved since recon: Jitter auth/role model shipped in PR #144; Institute suspended-login block shipped in PR #79; Jitter Sandra writeback shipped in PR #146 (Jitter) + PRs #145/#224 (Sandra), production executor proof PASS 2026-06-02. See the relevant app sections.\n\n## Recon first (read-only, per app)\n- [ ] **One code-review agent per app:** map v1 features → actual routes/components/API endpoints/tests/env vars.\n- [ ] **One integration-contract agent:** compare [[Jitter]]→[[Sandra]] and [[BMH Institute]]→[[Closer Lab]] payload/auth/origin expectations.\n- [ ] **One QA-\n\n### CURATED: mocs/MOC - v1.md\n---\ntype: moc\nstatus: active\ntags: [v1]\ncreated: 2026-05-30\nupdated: 2026-06-07\n---\n# MOC - v1\n\nDefinition of done = every box checked. Filtered by [[STRATEGY]]. Step-by-step detail behind each happy-path box → [[MOC - v1 Verification Steps]].\n\n## [[Sandra]] (CRM) — live\n- [x] Validate /properties filters on prod data — SHIPPED 2026-05-31 (PR #221, `8b9b238`): relationship-filter bugs fixed, Pass 1 272/272 + deployed-prod Pass 2 100/100. See [[Sandra Filter Fix Loop]].\n- [x] Happy path: import → filter prospects → run sequence → reply handled — PR #222 merged 2026-06-02; prod canaries and post-merge E2E `26797451702` passed\n- [x] Integration: receive [[Jitter]] writeback → log `call_activities` — [[Sandra]] PR #224 merged; production executor proof PASS 2026-06-02\n\n## [[Closer Lab]] — live\n- [x] Ship PR #60 — merged 2026-06-02; PR #61 already closed — superseded by PR #62\n- [x] Happy path: log in → run roleplay → get score — PR #60 merged 2026-06-02; post-fix main E2E and production smoke passed\n- [x] Integration: emit practice/call outcome → [[Sandra]] — PRs #71/#72 ([[Closer Lab]]) + PR #225 ([[Sandra]]) merged; production proof PASS 2026-06-02\n\n## [[BMH Institute]] — live\n- [x] Happy path: invite → learn → complete course — PR #79 merged 2026-06-02; seeded Playwright E2E green; cert PDF deferred post-v1\n- [ ] Integration: `course.completed` → [[Sandra]] contact\n\n## [[Jitter]] (dialer) — live\n- [x] Happy path: dial → disposition → recording — PR #144 merged 2026-06-02; capped live canary passed and CPS pacing is now merged/deployed through `334ef85`\n- [x] Integration: wire [[Sandra]] writeback — shipped in Sandra PR #145, Sandra PR #224, and Jitter boundary fix PR #146; deployed production executor proof wrote to Sandra `call_activities` on 2026-06-02\n- [x] v1 hardening: add auth/role model — PR #144 merged 2026-06-02 with Supabase auth, middleware, role RLS, and public webhook/executor exceptions\n- [ ] Final browser acceptance: (1) Telnyx carrier validation — **PASSED 2026-06-04** (real call rang +13107540662 with audio); (2) trust-boundary fix → browser-audio readiness contract → single-line live Telnyx dial proof; (3) Railway worker-backed unattended proof — that worker-backed proof is final acceptance\n\n### CURATED: _ai-context/CLAUDE-Setup.md\n---\ntype: meta\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-05-31\nupdated: 2026-05-31\ntags: [ai-os]\nrelated: ["[[Home]]", "[[conventions]]", "[[claude-codex-loop]]"]\n---\n# CLAUDE Setup — how the vault ↔ Claude integration works\n\nCompanion doc for the BMH-OS ↔ Claude Code wiring. Explains where memory lives, how every Claude tab boots with vault context, how to test the wiring, and how to keep it healthy. If you only read one thing: **the vault is the source of truth; everything else points into it.**\n\n## Canonical-memory model\n\n`~/BMH-OS` is the **single source of truth** for durable cross-session memory. Two other surfaces look like memory but are not — they are thin **pointers** into the vault:\n\n- The per-project auto-memory at `~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/MEMORY.md` — an index of one-line pointers, not the content itself.\n- The SessionStart **briefing** injected into every tab (see below) — pointers + status lines, never note bodies.\n\n**On any conflict, the vault wins.** A pointer that disagrees with the vault is stale; fix the pointer, trust the vault. This keeps a single write target: durable facts get promoted into `projects/ decisions/ contracts/ sources/` per [[conventions]], and the pointers are regenerated/refreshed to match — never the other way around.\n\n## How context loads in every tab\n\nTwo mechanisms, complementary:\n\n1. **SessionStart hook → briefing.** On every new Claude tab (any working directory), the SessionStart hook runs `~/BMH-OS/_meta/session-briefing.sh`. It emits a **lean** briefing assembled live from the vault:\n   - **Goal** and **Bottleneck** pulled from `STRATEGY.md` (the "One obsessional goal" and "The bottleneck right now" sections).\n   - **Active now:** the first few `- ` items from `_Active.md`.\n   - **Projects:** each note in `projects/` with its `status:`.\n   - **Recent captures:** the 4 newest `_inbox/*.md` titles.\n   The script is **fail-soft** (`set -uo pipefail`, exits 0 if the vault is missing) so a broken vault never blocks a session. It drains and ignores the hook JSON on stdin.\n\n2. **Root `CLAUDE.md` auto-load (vault cwd only).** When a Claude session\'s working directory is the vault (`~/BMH-OS`), Claude Code auto-loads the root `CLAUDE.md` in full — the complete protocol. Outside the vault you get the lean briefing instead, which points you back to `~/BMH-OS/CLAUDE.md` if you need the full protocol.\n\nThe division is deliberate: the briefing is the **everywhere, lightweight** signal; the full `CLAUDE.md` is the **in-vault\n\n### CURATED: _ai-context/claude-codex-loop.md\n---\ntype: meta\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-05-31\nupdated: 2026-05-31\ntags: [ai-os]\nrelated: ["[[_Active-Pairs]]", "[[STRATEGY]]", "[[CLAUDE-Setup]]"]\n---\n# Claude ↔ Codex convergence loop\n\nThe formal protocol for the autonomous, adversarial Claude↔Codex loop. Claude **plans, reviews, and independently verifies**; Codex **executes everything** with hands on the computer. The loop cycles until the goal is proven complete or a real blocker stops it. Referencing Codex here is correct — it is the subject of this protocol.\n\nCanonical source: the Codex skill `~/.codex/skills/custom-convergence-loop/SKILL.md`. This note formalizes it for the vault and names the BMH-OS conventions (pairing, handoff blocks, loop history) layered on top.\n\n## Roles\n\n- **Claude = brain / orchestrator / reviewer / bridge-driver.** Plans the goal, defines acceptance gates, reviews every Codex report, and **independently verifies** with its own tools (reads files, runs checks, opens the browser) rather than trusting Codex\'s word. Claude holds the merge gate. Claude also **drives the handoff bridge** — it delivers each EXECUTE into Codex and pulls the REPORT back (via `custom-codex-watcher`; see Transport). Claude is advisory on *what* to do; it is authoritative on *whether it\'s done*.\n- **Codex = executor / hands.** Does all **task** actuation — file edits, commands, git, browser proof — for the work itself. (The cross-app *handoff* is driven by Claude, not Codex — see Transport.) Codex **adversarially refutes** each instruction before executing it.\n\nClaude is not the first reviewer of Codex\'s code: once a diff exists, Codex runs `custom-manual-code-review` (with reviewer sub-agents) and fixes valid findings **before** escalating to Claude for review or a next-step decision.\n\n## Entry — Claude-initiated\n\nJarrad kicks the loop off from the Claude side by running the Claude skill **`custom-start-orchestration`** (the renamed + extended kickoff). That skill:\n\n1. Plans the goal and maps it to a `plan_source` (repo plan / roadmap / issue / PR / thread).\n2. Defines a stable **`goal_id`** and explicit, independently verifiable **acceptance gates**.\n3. Registers the **paired sessions** (see Pairing) in [[_Active-Pairs]].\n4. Emits the first **EXECUTE** handoff block to Codex.\n\nIf acceptance gates are missing, derive a conservative gate list from the goal and confirm it before accepting any `DONE`.\n\n## Pairing & naming\n\nEach loop runs as a 1:1 pair of sessions, titled by `goal_id` with a role suff\n\n### CURATED: _ai-context/research-memory-and-model-specificity.md\n---\ntype: source\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-05-31\nupdated: 2026-05-31\ntags: [ai-os]\nrelated: ["[[CLAUDE-Setup]]"]\n---\n# Research: Memory architecture & Claude model specificity\n\nOngoing research area. Two themes that govern how the AI OS *remembers* and\n*which brain* it uses. Not live web research — this note defines the area and\nparks open threads as **Question / Idea / Status (open)**. Setup state lives in\n[[CLAUDE-Setup]].\n\n---\n\n## Theme A — Memory architecture\n\nThere are several distinct memory layers in play. They are easy to conflate,\nand conflating them is how notes get duplicated and contradict each other. The\nresearch goal is to keep their *roles* crisp and make the vault the single\ncanonical store.\n\n### The layers\n\n1. **`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` — global behavior.** How Claude *acts* across every\n   project: response format, pushback stance, worktree hygiene, model-switch\n   nudges. Behavioral policy, not facts about the business. Hand-authored,\n   rarely auto-written.\n2. **`~/.claude/projects/.../MEMORY.md` (+ linked notes) — auto-memory.** A\n   per-project index of durable facts/decisions the assistant accumulates\n   (app-launch status, the filter-fix loop, the memory daemon). Convenient and\n   close to the runtime, but it is *Claude-runtime-local* — not the canonical\n   business store, and not visible to Codex or other tools.\n3. **`~/BMH-OS` vault — canonical store.** The durable, tool-agnostic source of\n   truth: projects, decisions, contracts, sources, MOCs, under [[conventions]].\n   Linked markdown, no Dataview. This is what *should* win when layers disagree.\n4. **SessionStart hooks — the bridge.** Inject vault context into a fresh Claude\n   session at startup (see [[CLAUDE-Setup]] and the briefing-tuning thread in\n   [[research-vault-integration]]). The hook is plumbing, not storage — it\n   *carries* memory from the vault into context; it doesn\'t *hold* it.\n\n### How they relate (the canonical-vault model)\n\n- **Behavior** lives in `CLAUDE.md` (layer 1).\n- **Facts/decisions** belong in the **vault** (layer 3) — canonical.\n- **Auto-memory** (layer 2) is a fast scratch/cache close to the Claude runtime;\n  anything durable there should be *promoted* into the vault, not left as the\n  authority.\n- **Hooks** (layer 4) move layer-3 content into a session; they should read from\n  the vault, never become a parallel store.\n\n**Question.** Where is the boundary between auto-memory (layer 2) and the vault\n(layer 3)? When does an auto-memory fact graduate t\n\n### CURATED: _ai-context/research-vault-integration.md\n---\ntype: source\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-05-31\nupdated: 2026-05-31\ntags: [ai-os]\nrelated: ["[[CLAUDE-Setup]]", "[[claude-codex-loop]]"]\n---\n# Research: Vault ↔ Claude integration\n\nOngoing research area. Open questions and directions for deepening how the\n`~/BMH-OS` vault feeds Claude (and Codex) and how Claude writes back. Not live\nweb research — this note *defines* the area and parks open threads. Each entry\nis **Question / Idea / Status (open)**. Setup state lives in [[CLAUDE-Setup]];\nthe autonomous wake loop lives in [[claude-codex-loop]].\n\n## 1. MCP bundles for vault access\n\n**Question.** Should vault reads/writes go through a dedicated MCP server\n(filesystem-scoped to `~/BMH-OS`, or an Obsidian-specific MCP) instead of raw\n`Read`/`Write`/`Bash`? What does an MCP buy us that file tools don\'t — link\ngraph queries, tag-aware search, frontmatter validation, orphan detection at\nwrite time?\n\n**Idea.** A thin "vault MCP" exposing a small verb set: `search(query, tag?)`,\n`get(note)`, `backlinks(note)`, `write(note, frontmatter, body)` with the\n[[conventions]] orphan-gate + tag-lint enforced server-side so no agent can\nwrite an invalid note. Could wrap the existing `_meta/tag-lint.py`. Evaluate\nthe community Obsidian MCP servers vs. a purpose-built one before committing.\n\n**Status (open).** No vault MCP today; agents use plain file tools. Decide:\nadopt existing MCP, build thin custom, or stay on file tools and lean on\nconventions + lint as the guardrail.\n\n## 2. Tuning the SessionStart briefing\n\n**Question.** What belongs in the SessionStart hook injection, and what should\nbe pulled on demand instead? The briefing must stay lean as the vault grows —\na hook that dumps the whole vault defeats itself.\n\n**Idea.** Tier the briefing: (a) always inject — `Home.md`, `_Active.md`,\ncurrent strategy pointer, the convention/tag rules; (b) inject a *manifest*\n(titles + one-line summaries + paths) rather than full note bodies, so Claude\nknows what exists and fetches the body only when relevant; (c) never inject —\narchived notes, raw `_inbox/` captures, session logs. Watch the token budget\nof the hook output as a first-class metric; set a ceiling and alert when the\nmanifest approaches it.\n\n**Status (open).** Briefing exists (see [[CLAUDE-Setup]]); no formal tiering or\ntoken ceiling yet. Open: define the manifest format and the inject/exclude\nrules as the vault scales past a few dozen notes.\n\n## 3. Surviving context compaction\n\n**Question.** When a long session compacts,\n\n### DECISION/CLI-over-GUI wake plane mailbox architecture.md\n---\ntype: decision\nstatus: active\ntags: [session, auto-capture]\ncreated: 2026-06-01\nupdated: 2026-06-01\nrelated: ["[[STRATEGY]]", "[[Home]